Multifunctional \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) nanosheets derived from 3D metal–organic frameworks for mild-temperature-photothermal-sonodynamic-chemodynamic therapy under photoacoustic image guidance
Xiao Han a,1, Chen Zhao a,1, Shanshan Wang a, ZhengYi Pan b, Zhenqi Jiang a,∗, Xiaoying Tang a,∗
a School of Medical Technology, School of Life Science, Institute of Engineering Medicine, School of Chemistry and Chemical Engineering, Analysis & Testing Center, Beijing Institute of Technology, Beijing 100081, China b Beijing University of Chemical Technology, Beijing 100081, China
G R A P H I C A L A B S T R A C T
## A R T I C L E I N F O
## A B S T R A C T
Article history:  Received 14 February 2022  Revised 10 April 2022  Accepted 11 April 2022  Available online 14 April 2022
Keywords:  Metal-organic frameworks  \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)  Mild photothermal therapy  Photoacoustic imaging  Sonodynamic therapy
The synthesis of composite nanosheets with multiple functions remains significantly challenging. In this study, we developed multifunctional \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\)-PEG nanosheets as theranostic agents combining sonodynamic therapy and a low-power-mild-photothermal-enhanced Fenton effect. The two-dimensional \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) composite nanosheets were synthesized by reducing metal–organic frameworks (MOF)-derived from \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) composite nanoparticles (NPs) by hydrogen. The resulting \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) was introduced into polyethylene (PEG) to improve water solubility and increase the size of the particles to allow a higher level of \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) accumulation at the tumor site through the enhanced permeability and retention effect. The \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) nanosheets had excellent sonodynamic therapy (SDT) effects and could also achieve low-power mild photothermal therapy guided by photoacoustic imaging with high photothermal conversion efficiency \((\eta=56.2\%)\). Also, a strong photothermal effect accelerated the rate of the Fenton reaction to enhance the efficacy of chemodynamic therapy (CDT). Importantly, combination therapy can achieve significant antitumour efficacy whilst the strong biocompatibility of the particles minimising adverse effects in normal cells. This study provides a promising strategy for the development of novel two-dimensional composite materials that are safe and have multifunctional diagnostic and therapeutic properties.
