## 1. Introduction
Two-dimensional materials such as graphene, black phosphorus, and MXene have broad applications in tumor diagnosis and
treatment due to their large surface areas, unique surface chemistry and quantum size effects [1–5]. In comparison to traditional granular materials, two-dimensional materials often have stronger therapeutic efficacy based on the ability to produce higher levels of singlet oxygen and more efficient light-to-heat conversion [6–11]. Two-dimensional materials can be prepared using several methods including mechanical exfoliation, ion-intercalation and exfoliation, liquid-phase exfoliation, and chemical vapor deposition (CVD) [12–15]. These methods often require strong acids, have low yields, and introduce other elements in the synthesis process [16]. Whilst these methods can be use to effectively prepare two-dimensional materials, the direct synthesis of two-dimensional composite materials such as \(\mathsf{C u}_{2}0_{2}\) graphene remains challenging as they are usually adsorbed on the surface of the material [17]. These combinations are often not sufficiently stable which limits their development and applications in biomedicine.
MOFs are porous materials that have been widely studied for applications as biosensors and in diagnostic and treatment due to their strong biocompatibility, high carrying capacity, and high rate of metabolic clearance [18–22]. The morphology of MOF can be adjusted by controlling the synthesis conditions (such as the addition of surfactants) to prepare two-dimensional MOF nanosheets [23–26]. Previously studies have reported that two-dimensional (2D) MOF nanosheets have improved diagnostic and therapeutic effects compared to nano MOF particles [27,28]. MOF-derived metal/C materials have been widely studied in applications including the production of batteries, catalysts, separation media and sensing technologies due to their in-situ synthesis method and stable chemical structures [29–33]. Nanoparticles obtained by calcination often have a higher specific surface area and a porous structure making them attractive candidates for biomedical applications [34–37]. Studies have demonstrated the synthesis of carbon-encapsulated nanoparticles through MOF-derived techniques by adjusting annealing conditions [38,39]. Chen et al. synthesized an efficient \(\mathsf{C u C o/C}\) catalyst using one-step carbonization of \(\mathsf{C u@C o-M O F}\) and studied the performance of the catalyst at different carbonization temperatures. When the pyrolysis temperature was \(900\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), the catalyst showed the best degradation efficiency [40]. These studies indicated that MOF-derived nanoparticles may be prepared by adjusting the synthesis conditions to prepare metal oxides/carbon composites, and 2D carbon nanosheets could be also synthesized from 2D MOF sheets [41–43]. Two-dimensional metal oxide/carbon nanosheets prepared by this method are likely to be more stable than those prepared by traditional methods and may have potential value in biomedical applications. However, relatively few studies have reported on the synthesis and biomedical applications of nano MOF-derived from 2D metal oxide/carbon nanosheets through the MOF-derived to 2D metal oxide/carbon nanosheets from nano MOF.
In this study, we present an effective method for the preparation of two-dimensional \(\mathrm{H}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) composite nanosheets by reducing MOF-derived \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) composite nanoparticles by hydrogen Scheme. 1. The resulting nanosheets were used for highefficiency mild-temperature photothermal/acoustic dynamic/ chemo dynamic therapy. Firstly, MIL-125(Ti)– \(\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) was introduced as a sacrificial template to calcinate \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) nanoparticles which were then reduced in hydrogen atmosphere to obtain two- dimensional \(\mathrm{H}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) nanosheets. The nanosheets were \(40-\) \(70\ \mathrm{nm}\) wide and \(4.25\ \mathrm{nm}\) thick. Through X-ray diffraction (XRD) and ED analysis, we found that the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) on the surface of the obtained nanosheets was transformed into amorphous \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) . The nanosheets had excellent reactive oxygen species (ROS) generating properties with a high photothermal conversion efficiency and photoacoustic imaging performance. Also, the \(\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}\) produced by the reduction reaction could convert the elevated levels of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) in the tumor microenvironment onto ·OH.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Synthesis of \(H{\mathrm{-TiO}}_{2}/C\) -PEG nanosheets
The first step in the synthesis of the \(\mathrm{H}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/C\) -PEG nanosheets was the synthesis of MIL-125(Ti)– \(\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) [44]. 80 mmol of 2- aminoterephthalic acid \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -BDC), 400 mmol of methyl benzoic acid and 80 mmol of tetra butyl titanate were added to an \(80\;\mathrm{mL}\) N,N-Dimethylformamide (DMF) and \(40\,\mathrm{mL}\) methanol mixing solution. When the solution was cleaned, it was transferred to a \(150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) reactor for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) , purified four times by ethanol and then dried under a vacuum. The obtained product \((500\;\mathrm{mg})\) was heated to \(350\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\,(2\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min})\) for \(2\,\mathrm{h}\) in a tube furnace under air. After cooling down to the room temperature and under a \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) atmosphere \((5{\cdot}95,\,\mathrm{v/v})\) , the tube was heated to \(500\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) \((5\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min})\) for \(4\,\mathrm{h}\) . After cooling, a black product was obtained and named \(\mathrm{H}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/C\) (about \(120~\mathrm{mg})\) . \(\mathrm{H}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/C\) particles \(\left(100\mathrm{~mg}\right)\) were collected and stirred with mPEG- \(\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) \(\left(300\;\mathrm{mg}\right)\) for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) . Finally, the samples were collected and freeze-dried after centrifugation \((170~\mathrm{mg})\) .
