## 2.3. Cellular uptake of \(H{\mathrm{-TiO}}_{2}/C\) -PEG in vitro
To quantitatively measure the content of Ti in 4 T1 cells, the cells were incubated with \(\mathrm{N-TiO}_{2}/C\) -PEG \((50~\upmu\mathrm{g/ml)}\) solution for another 1, 2, 4, 8, and \(12~\mathrm{{h}}\) . After then, cells were collected and re-suspended in PBS \((1\ \times\ 10^{5}\) cells/mL) for ICP-OES (Optima 2100, Perkin-Elmer, USA).
## 2.4. ROS generation of \(H{\mathrm{-TiO}}_{2}/C\) -PEG in vitro
ROS generation was also assessed in vitro on 4 T1 cells. The singlet oxygen, superoxide and hydroxyl radicals were detected by SOSG and coumarin-3-carboxylic acid, respectively, using confocal laser scanning microscope (CLSM) imaging. Briefly, 4 T1 cells were seeded in a \(3.5{\mathrm{-cm}}\) petri dish and cultured overnight. The cells were incubated with \(\mathrm{H}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/C\) -PEG or PBS at a Ti concentration of \(20~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}\) for \(4\,\mathrm{h}\) , then washed with PBS three times to remove excess \(\mathrm{H}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/C\) -PEG. Cells were stained with respective dye and incubated for \(15~\mathrm{\min}\) . The cells were irradiated with light \((1064\ \mathrm{nm},\,0.75\ \mathrm{W/cm}^{2},\,5\ \mathrm{min})\) or US \((1.0\ \mathrm{MHz},\,1\ \mathrm{W\cm}^{-2},\,5\ \mathrm{min})\) and both of light and US. Then the cells were washed with PBS three times to remove excess ROS probes and CLSM was immediately used to visualize various ROS species generated in live cells by detecting the fluorescence signals inside the cells. The total ROS wwas also analyzed by flow cytometry with H2DCFDA assay.
## 2.5. Cell cytotoxicity assays
2.6. Determination of the antitumor activity in vitro
The 4 T1 cells \((100\,\upmu\mathrm{L};8\times10^{4}\,\mathrm{cells/mL})\) were incubated in a 96- well plate for \(16~\mathrm{h}\) , enabling them to adhere to the bottom of the plate. Then, the culture medium was replaced with \(100~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of DMEM medium containing PBS and \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) -PEG (5, 10, 20, 40, and \(80~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}\) for Ti) and incubated for another \(^{8\mathrm{~h~}}\) . After rinsing with PBS, the cells were treated with the following: (1) \(\mathrm{US}+\) Light, (2) \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\,+\) Light, (3) \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C-PEG}\,+\,\mathrm{US},\) (4) H\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C-PEG}@~+~\mathrm{US}~+~\mathrm{Light},\) (5) Control [US \((0.6\mathrm{~W/cm}^{2}\) , 1 MHz, \(3\;\mathrm{min}\) , \(50\;\%\) duty), Light ( \(1064\;\mathrm{nm}\) , \(0.75\;\mathrm{W/cm}^{2}\) )]. Then, the optical density was determined at a wavelength of \(450\;\mathrm{nm}\) using a microplate reader (Multiskan FC, Thermo Fisher, U.S.A.). CLSM images were obtained from Leica confocal laser fluorescence microscope (TSCSPS II, Germany) after different treatment. The aptosis of cells were also anlsysised by Annexin V-FITC/PI by flow cytometry.
## 2.7. Pharmacokinetics
For the pharmacokinetic study, the rats were treated with \(100~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) -PEG (Ti concentration of \(0.4\,\mathrm{\mg/mL})\) intravenously, and the blood samples \((500\,\upmu\mathrm{L})\) were collected at different times. The plasma was collected by centrifugation then tested by ICP-OES.
## 2.8. Biodistribution of \(H{\mathrm{-TiO}}_{2}/C\) -PEG nanosheets in vivo
All of the experimental protocols involving animals were approved by the Regional Ethics Committee for Animal Experiments at the Beijing Institute of Technology (Permit No. SYXK Jing 2017– 0031). 4 T1 tumor-bearing mice were intravenously injected with \(100~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) -PEG nanosheets at an equivalent Ti concentra
tion of \(0.4~\mathrm{mg/mL}\) . The major organs and tumors from these mice were harvested after \(24~\mathrm{{h}}\) and evaluated via inductively coupled plasma-optical emission spectrometry (ICP-OES).
## 2.9. Anti-tumor effects in vivo
Female \(\mathtt{B A L B/c}\) nude mice (6–8 weeks old; Peking University) were used for the animal studies. Mice were randomly assigned to the following six treatment groups \(\left(\mathfrak{n}=5\right)\) : (1) phosphate buffer saline (PBS), (2) Light \(^+\) ultrasound (US), (3) \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C-PEG}\) , (4) H\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C-PEG}\,+\,\mathrm{US},\) (5) \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C-PEG}\,+\,\mathrm{Light},\) and (6) \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C-PE}\) \(\mathrm{G}~+~\mathrm{Light}~+~\mathrm{US}\) . After 14 days, the tumors had reached a size \(6\sim50~\mathrm{mm}^{3}\) and the mice were then treated intravenously with samples at a Ti concentration of \(0.4~\mathrm{mg/mL}\) . The mice were anesthetized \(24~\mathrm{{h}}\) after injection and the tumors were exposed to US irradiation or \(1064~\mathrm{nm}\) of light for appropriate times \((0.6\;\mathrm{W/cm}^{2}\) , duty rate \(50\;\%\), 3 min for US and \(0.75\;\mathrm{W/cm}^{2}\) , 5 min). The temperature change of tumor under light was observed by Flirone \(@\) Pro (FLIR, U.S.). The body weights and tumor volumes [volume \(=\mathsf{a b}^{2}/2]\) were monitored every other day. After 14 days, the mice were euthanized and the tumors were collected and imaged. The tumors were stained with hematoxylin and eosin (H&E), TUNEL, and Ki67. ( \(\mathbf{\dot{a}}=\) tumor length, \(\mathbf{b}=\) tumor width).
## 2.10. Toxicity evaluation of \(H{\mathrm{-TiO}}_{2}/C\) -PEG in vivo
To evaluate the toxicity of \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) -PEG in vivo, four groups \(\mathtt{B a l b/c}\) mice was used (five each group), then all the mice were treated with \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) -PEG \((100~\upmu\mathrm{L})\) Ti concentration of \(0.4~\mathrm{mg/}\) ml) or PBS via intravenous injection for one time. The survival rate and body weight were recorded for 14 days. To understand the toxicity in vivo deeply, another group treated mice were sacrificed on
the 1st, 7th, and 14 th day. The collected blood was analyzed by blood analyzer (Sysmex XT-1800i, Japan), and Hitachi 7600–110 autoanalyzer (Hitachi, Tokyo, Japan), and the major organs were collected then stained with hematoxylin and Eosin (H&E).
## 2.11. Characterization
The DLS and zeta potential were measured at room temperature using a particle analyzer (Nano-2S, Malvern, England) in water at \(178^{\circ}\). Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and High-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) images were detected on a JEOL2100 (JEOL, Japan) at \(120~\mathrm{kV}\). The structural properties of samples were investigated by X-ray diffraction (XRD) using an X-ray powder diffractometer (XRD, D8 Discover, Bruker AXS). The concentration of elements of samples was analyzed by ICP-OES (Optima 2100, Perkin-Elmer, USA). Confocal laser scanning microscope (CLSM) images were acquired from Leica confocal laser fluorescence microscope (TSCSPS II, Germany). Photocoustic images were captured by VEVO LAZR-X imaging system (Fujifilm VisualSonics, USA). The above characterization was followed by the guidance of the company without change.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Synthesis and characterization of \(H\)-TiO\(_2\)/C-PEG nanosheets
To synthesize the \(H\)-TiO\(_2\)/C nanosheets, MIL-125(Ti)–\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) was introduced as a sacrificial template to calcinate \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) nanoparticles that were then reduced in a hydrogen atmosphere to obtain two-dimensional \(H\)-TiO\(_2\)/C composite nanosheets. The morphology of MIL-125(Ti)–\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), \(N\)-TiO\(_2\)/C and \(H\)-TiO\(_2\)/C nanosheets was characterized by transmission electron microscopy (TEM), XRD and high-resolution TEM (HR-TEM). The diameters of MIL-125(Ti)–\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), \(N\)-TiO\(_2\)/C and \(H\)-TiO\(_2\)/C were \(40{-}70~\mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. 1a, 1b and 1d). XRD results of MIL-125(Ti)–\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) and \(N\)-TiO\(_2\)/C showed that the precursor had a crystal structure (Fig. S1). HR-TEM images showed that the \(N\)-TiO\(_2\)/C had clear lattice structures with a fringe spacing of \(0.352~\mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. 1c) that was associated with the basal spacing of (1 0 1), the lattice plane of the monoclinic anatase \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\). However, HR-TEM images and the electron diffraction (ED) pattern of \(H\)-\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) nanosheets (Fig. 1e and 1f) showed an amorphous structure. Energy dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) mapping images indicated that C, N, O and Ti were uniformly distributed throughout the whole macroporous \(H\)-TiO\(_2\)/C nanosheets (Fig. 1g). The Raman spectrum of \(H\)-TiO\(_2\)/C is shown in Fig. S2. The peaks observed at 1340 (D band) \(\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and 1576 (R band) \(\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) suggested the presence of amorphous carbon. To better investigate the morphology of \(H\)-TiO\(_2\)/C nanosheets, 3D images and atomic force microscope (AFM) characterization of \(H\)-TiO\(_2\)/C nanosheets were acquired. As shown in Fig. 1h and 1i, the height of the \(H\)-TiO\(_2\)/C nanosheets was \(4.25~\mathrm{nm}\).
The \(H\)-TiO\(_2\)/C-PEG nanosheets were synthesized by physical adsorption. The hydrodynamic size of the nanosheets was approximately \(143~\mathrm{nm}\) based on the particle size distributions and the Zeta-potential was \(-6.2\pm0.7~\mathrm{mV}\) as shown in Fig. 2a. The Zeta-potential of the \(H\)-TiO\(_2\)/C was \(3.4\pm0.6~\mathrm{mV}\). These properties may prolong circulation times in the blood. The stability of the \(H\)-TiO\(_2\)/C-PEG nanosheets was confirmed by dynamic light scattering (DLS) and the data are presented in Fig. S3. When soaked in PBS and fetal bovine serum (FBS) \((10\;\mathrm{wt}\%)\) for 14 days, the particle size of the \(H\)-TiO\(_2\)/C-PEG nanosheets did not significantly change. The low level of Ti released from the \(H\)-TiO\(_2\)/C-PEG nanosheets also proved that the material was stable (Fig. S4). The XRD results shown in Fig. 2b indicated that the \(H\)-TiO\(_2\)/C-PEG nanosheets were amorphous which was similar to the results obtained from the HR-TEM and ED pattern. The \(H\)-TiO\(_2\)/C-PEG nanosheets showed high levels of absorbance from 500 to \(1100~\mathrm{nm}\). The amount of Ti was measured by the absorption at \(1064~\mathrm{nm}\) with a molar extinction coefficient of \(10.6~\mathrm{L}/(\mathrm{g}^{\ast}\mathrm{cm})\) (Fig. 2c and S5), which was higher than previous reports[45–47]. The high absorbance of \(H\)-TiO\(_2\)/C-PEG indicated a possible increase in temperature when stimulated with a \(1064~\mathrm{nm}\) laser.
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was used to explore the chemical states and the presence of Ti. The full-pattern spectra of the \(H\)-TiO\(_2\)/C nanosheets indicated the presence of \(\mathrm{O}_{1s}\), \(\mathrm{Ti}_{2p}\), \(\mathrm{N}_{1s}\) and \(\mathrm{C}_{1s}\). High-resolution XPS spectra were used to confirm the chemical balance of Ti ions in the \(H\)-TiO\(_2\)/C. Two spin–orbit doublets were observed at Ti-2p\(_{1/2}\) 463.38 eV and Ti-2p\(_{3/2}\) 457.82 eV, and Ti \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\) 464.58 eV and Ti \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) 458.98 eV, corresponding to \(\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}\) and \(\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}\) ions, respectively (Fig. 2e). The atom ratio of \(\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}\) and \(\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}\) was 6.4. As shown in Fig. 2f, the signals of the \(\mathrm{N}_{1s}\) orbit were fitted to three peaks at 401.61 eV (N-Ti), 400.46 eV (O—N—Ti) and 399.07 eV (Ti-N-O\(_2\)). These data indicated the successful doping of N[48]. The XPS image of \(\mathrm{O}_{1s}\) was fitted into two binding energies at 533.46 eV (C-OH) and 531.93 eV (C=O) and the C1s were divided into three peaks at 288.8 eV (C=O), 286.36 eV (C—O) and 284.8 eV (C—C) (Fig. S6). The XPS results showed that elemental N was doped into the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) structure that could increase the production of ROS. Also, the existence of \(\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}\) showed the expected increase in the Fenton-like reaction. These data demonstrate the successful synthesis of \(H\)-TiO\(_2\)/C nanosheets and their potential to improve therapeutic efficacy using US and laser activation.
