3.3. Cellular uptake and inhibition of \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\)-PEG nanosheets in vitro
The efficient cellular uptake of nanoparticles is a prerequisite for effective tumor treatment. To study the intracellular uptake of the \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\)-PEG nanosheets, 4 T1 cells were incubated with \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\)-PEG nanosheets \((50~\upmu\mathrm{g/mL})\) for 1, 2, 4, 8, and \(12~\mathrm{h}\). As shown in Fig. 4a, with increasing incubation times, the Ti content in the cells continuously increased indicating time-dependent cellular uptake that correlated with the performance of the \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\)-PEG nanosheets[50]. We then investigated the temperature changes
in 4 T1 cells incubated with \(50\ \upmu\mathrm{g/mL}\) of \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\)-PEG nanosheets in aqueous solution for \(8\,\mathrm{h}\) at different time points (from 0 to 300 s) under laser irradiation at \(0.75\,\mathrm{W/cm}^{2}\). Fig. 4b-c shows a rapid increase in temperature as a function of time.
The intracellular distribution of the \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\)-PEG nanosheets and ROS generation performance were observed from CLSM images (Fig. 4d). After incubation for \(8\,\mathrm{h}\), the \(\mathrm{US}+\) laser irradiation, \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\)-PEG nanosheets and the \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\)-PEG nanosheets + laser irradiation groups showed weak intracellular ROS-related fluorescence intensity. In contrast, the \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\)-PEG nanosheets + US and the \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\)-PEG nanosheets + US + laser irradiation groups showed effective intracellular ROS generation performance (4e-f). These data validated the mechanism of \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\)-PEG nanosheets as ultrasound sensitizers.
Based on the demonstrated therapeutic potential of \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\)-PEG in vitro, a series of experiments were conducted at the cellular level. As shown in Fig. 5a, the survival rates of MCF-10A and 4 T1
cells were \(80\,\%\) and \(95\,\%\) respectively after incubated with \(\mathrm{H-TiO_{2}/}\) C-PEG nanosheets for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) at \(80\,\mathrm{mg/mL}\) . These data indicated a high level of biocompatibility in normal cells and effective chemodynamic therapy in cancer cells due to the production of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) from the \(\mathrm{H-TiO_{2}/C}\) -PEG nanosheets. When laser and US irradiation were performed, the viability of the 4 T1 cells significantly decreased to \(10\,\%\) , indicating excellent photothermal therapy (PTT)-enhanced SDT performance (Fig. 5b). These results were further confirmed by live/dead cell tests as shown in Fig. 5c. Our data showed that \(\mathrm{H-TiO_{2}/C}\) -PEG exhibits good photothermal performance, Fentonlike reactions and ROS generation capacity. These findings support the use of \(\mathrm{H-TiO_{2}/C}\) -PEG nanosheets as antitumor agents. An apoptosis/necrosis assay was performed using annexin fluorescein isothiocyanate (FITC)/ propidium iodide (PI) double staining-based flow cytometry (Fig. 5d-e). \(\mathrm{US}+\) laser irradiation or incubation with \(\mathrm{H-TiO_{2}/C}\) -PEG alone caused negligible changes in cell viability, however, 4 T1 cells incubated with \(\mathrm{H-TiO_{2}/C}\) -PEG and treated with US irradiation or laser irradiation induced cell apoptosis. Notably, the proportion of cells that underwent apoptosis in the \(\mathrm{H-TiO_{2}/C-P E G+U S+L}\) group was \(82.9\pm3.6\;\%\) indicating that \(\mathrm{H-TiO_{2}/C}\) -PEG could induce apoptosis under laser or US activation
and supported the use of SDT/PTT/CDT in tumor cells. The results from the red blood cell hemolysis experiment were shown in Fig. S11. In contrast to the positive control group (added with water), no significant change in the the color of red blood cells was observed in the different groups suggesting the favorable hemocompatibility of \(\mathrm{H-TiO_{2}/C}\) -PEG.
## 3.4. Photoacoustic imaging and antitumor effects of \(\mathrm{H-TiO_{2}/C}\) -PEG nanosheets in vivo
Photoacoustic imaging was used to determine the optimum accumulation time for the \(\mathrm{H-TiO_{2}/C}\) -PEG nanosheets in tumors (Fig. 6a, b and S10). A strong photoacoustic signal was detected \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) after intravenous injection indicating sufficient accumulation of the \(\mathrm{H-TiO_{2}/C}\) -PEG at the tumor site. Also, we investigated the blood concentration of Ti and found that the concentration of H\(\mathrm{TiO_{2}/C}\) -PEG nanosheets in the blood decreased with time (Fig. 6c). The half-life in the circulation was \(5.072\pm0.476\mathrm{~h~}\) . The performance analysis of the \(\mathrm{H-TiO_{2}/C}\) -PEG nanosheets suggested good performance and retention through the enhanced permeability and retention effect.
We then investigated the organ coefficients of \(\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}\). As shown in Fig. 6d, \(24\,\mathrm{~h~}\) after intravenous injection of the \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) -PEG nanosheets, increased levels of \(\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}\) were observed in the liver which caused an immune response. Notably, high levels of Ti accumulated in the tumor \(24\,\mathrm{~h~}\) after injection at \(7.27\,\pm\,0.45\,\,\%\mathrm{D/g}\). These data indicated that the \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) -PEG nanosheets can effectively accumulate in tumors. The intratumoral temperatures in the experimental (intravenous injection of \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) -PEG nanosheets) and blank (intravenous injection of PBS) group were recorded using an infrared thermal camera. The intratumoral temperatures of the experimental group increased to \(48.5\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in \(5\,\mathrm{min}\) after laser irradiation \((1064\,\mathrm{nm},0.75\,\mathrm{W/cm}^{2})\), whilst the temperatures in the PBS group increased to \(37.3\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Fig. 6e, 6f). These data demonstrated the mild PTT effects of the \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) -PEG nanosheets.
We then evaluated the anticancer efficiency of the \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) -PEG nanosheets in 4 T1 tumour. Tumor-bearing mice were randomly divided into six groups: (i) Blank group (intravenous injection of PBS), (ii) \(\mathrm{US}+\) laser group (intravenous injection of PBS followed by US and laser irradiation), (iii) \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) -PEG nanosheets group (intravenous injection with \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) -PEG nanosheets), (iv) \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) -PEG nanosheets \(^+\) US group (intravenous injection with \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) -PEG nanosheets followed by US irradiation), (v) \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) -PEG nanosheets \(^+\) laser group (intravenous injection with \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) -PEG nanosheets then treatment with laser irradiation), (vi) \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) -PEG nanosheets \(^+\) US \(^+\) laser group (intravenous injection with \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) -PEG nanosheets followed by US and laser irradiation). The tumors were allowed to grow to an approximate size of \(80\,\mathrm{mm}^{3}\) before treatment and the tumor volumes were measured every other day.
As shown in Fig. 7a, the tumor volumes in the mice from groups i, ii and iii increased with time, whilst the growth of the tumors in groups iv and v were delayed and the tumors in group vi were eliminated. Also, we found that all of the mice in group vi were completely cured and showed no signs of recurrence after 60 days. However, tumors recurred in animals in the other experimental groups. Representative photographs of the mice are presented in Fig. 7c. The animals in groups iv and v showed signs of therapeutic efficacy and the animals in group vi showed inhibited tumor growth with no recurrence. H&E, K67 and tunnel staining were performed on tumor samples from each of the experimental groups. As shown in Fig. 7d, there were no significant differences in these biomarkers in groups i, ii and iii, whilst apoptosis was observed in groups iv and v. Tumor samples from group vi showed a dissociation of the nucleus. After 14 days of the different treatments, no obvious weight changes and signs of inflammation and injury to the main organs were observed in the animals from group i to vi (Fig. S12 and S13). These results indicated that the \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) -PEG nanosheets had no significant in vivo toxicity and demonstrated the PTT-enhanced SDT effects after US and laser irradiation. Overall, these data indicated that the \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) -PEG nanosheets have combined therapeutic effects guided by photoacoustic imaging and do not cause acute toxicity in mice.
## 3.5. Biocompatibility of \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) -PEG nanosheets in vivo
The body weights of the mice following different treatments were recorded every other day. As shown in Fig. 8a, no significant weight changes were observed in the different treatment groups indicating that the \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) -PEG nanosheets were biocompatible.
The organ coefficients of the tumor-bearing mice from the Blank group and \(\mathrm{H-TiO_{2}/C}\) -PEG nanosheets \(^+\) US \(^+\) laser group were shown in Fig. 8b. During the 14-day experiment, the organ coefficients of the PBS and the experimental groups did not change sig
nificantly, further demonstrating the safety of the \(\mathrm{H-TiO_{2}/C}\) -PEG nanosheets. Intravenous injection of the \(\mathrm{H-TiO_{2}/C}\) -PEG nanosheets did not trigger the immune response in the mice. Routine blood analysis showed that the white blood cells (WBC), red blood cells
(RBC), platelets (PLT), hemoglobin (HGB), hematocrit (HCT), mean corpuscular hemoglobin (MCH), mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration (MCHC) and mean corpuscular volume (MCV) were within the normal levels (Fig. 8c-e and S14)[19]. Kidney-damage indices, liver-damage indices, immune factors and the TNF- \(\cdot\propto\) levels in the mice treated with PBS and the \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) -PEG nanosheets are
shown in Fig. f-h and S15. These results further demonstrate the low toxicity of the \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) -PEG nanosheets. As shown in Fig. 8i, no inflammatory lesions and organ damage were found in the H&E stained tissue slices of the main organs of mice treated with PBS and the \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) -PEG nanosheets for 1, 7 and 14 days. Overall, the \(\mathrm{H-TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) -PEG nanosheets had excellent biocompatibility and safety.
## 4. Conclusions
4. ConclusionsRecently, two-dimensional nanosheets have made good progress in oncology treatment, but most of the current two-dimensional sheets originate from organic liquid phase exfoliation or acid etching[3,9,10]. Due to the strong adsorption of 2D nanosheets, organic solvents or acid solutions that are not removed may cause in vivo toxicity. Therefore, it is beneficial to explore a non-liquid phase synthesis method to promote the application of 2D nanosheets in oncology treatment. In this work, we have successfully designed and prepared a multifunctional \(\mathrm{H-TiO_{2}/C}\) -PEG theranostic platform for sonodynamic/photothermal/chemody name synergistic treatment under the guidance of photoacoustic imaging. The multifunctional agent was synthesized through hydrogen reduction of MOF-derived \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}\) composite nanoparticles that yielded extremely high levels of ROS (type I and type II) under US than commercial \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and had outstanding photothermal performance with laser activation, the the photothermal conversion efficiency of the \(\mathrm{H-TiO_{2}/C}\) -PEG nanosheets is \(56.2~\%\) and it was higher than previously reported[45–47]. The nanosheets had remarkable impacts on chemodynamic therapy caused by the Fenton effect and excellent photoacoustic imaging properties. Also, the \(\mathrm{H-TiO_{2}/C}\) -PEG nanosheets effectively eliminated cancer cells through the combination of mild-temperature photothermal/ acoustic dynamic/chemo dynamic therapy under the guidance of excellent photoacoustic imaging properties. This approach enables the diverse functions of \(\mathrm{H-TiO_{2}/C}\) -PEG agents for diagnosis and therapy. The two-dimensional \(\mathrm{H-TiO_{2}/C}\) -PEG agenst accumulated in tumors through the EPR effect to increased the effective therapeutic concentration at the tumor site. This work proposes a novel strategy for the synthesis of MOF-derived 2D metal oxide/carbon nanosheets and demonstarters their potential as therapeutic agents in the treatment of cancer.
